```
@id("close-workorder-main-with-recent-human-approval")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    output.decision: "APPROVED",
    output.approver_kind: "human"
  }
};

// The approval lookup must itself be permitted, or it is never recorded as a response.
@id("allow-authority-approval-lookup")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval", resource);

@id("allow-readonly-list-workorders")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"list_workorders", resource);

@id("allow-readonly-get-workorder")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"get_workorder", resource);

@id("allow-readonly-get-workorder-tasks")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_tasks", resource);

@id("allow-readonly-get-workorder-costs")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_costs", resource);

@id("allow-readonly-get-workorder-actuals-vs-planned")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned", resource);

@id("allow-readonly-get-workorder-kpis")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_kpis", resource);

@id("allow-readonly-get-schedule-calendar")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"get_schedule_calendar", resource);

@id("allow-readonly-get-my-assigned-workorders")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"get_my_assigned_workorders", resource);

@id("allow-readonly-get-failure-codes")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"get_failure_codes", resource);

@id("never-approve-workorders")
forbid (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"approve_workorder", resource);
```

I had to guess two string values: `output.decision` is written as `"APPROVED"` and `output.approver_kind` as `"human"`. The manifest declares both as plain strings and does not list their possible values. If the authority server returns different values, such as lowercase `"approved"` or `"HUMAN"`, change them in the close policy, or it will deny every close.

Closing is permitted only at `MAIN`. It also requires a successful `authority_get_approval` response from the last hour, for the same `wonum` and `site_id`, showing an approval by a human. A denied or failed lookup is recorded as an error event, so it does not satisfy this condition. Everything else is denied by default, including `update_workorder`, `assign_technician`, `cancel_workorder` and `generate_work_order`. `approve_workorder` is also explicitly forbidden.
